  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mi>mol</mi></math> of an organic compound containing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></math> carbon was burnt completely, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;was produced. The molar mass of compound is _____&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>gmol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> (Nearest integer)",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "200",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math> is the molar mass of the compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mol</mi></mrow></mfenced></math><br />mass of compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mi>mole</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>molecular</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math></p><p>mass of carbon <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>60</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;....(i)</p><p>moles of carbon <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>compound</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>molecular</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>carbon</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow><mn>12</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>60</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp; ....(ii)</p><p>Thus, moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;from combustion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mn>44</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo></math>0.1 moles of carbon dioxide</p><p>Using POAC,<br />Thus, from (i) and (ii),<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow><mn>12</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>60</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mn>44</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mn>44</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>100</mn><mn>60</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mol</mi></math></p>",
